Yes. Being seen is a near-universal hunger, but attention is finite—and the systems mediating us reward expression more than reception. Everyone broadcasts evidence of their existence; few feel witnessed, so they broadcast harder.

Listening requires temporary surrender of the center. You let another person’s model occupy working space without immediately correcting, competing, diagnosing, or converting it into your own story. That can feel like disappearance to someone already starved for recognition.

A real conversation needs a rhythm:

\text{Receive} \rightarrow \text{Reflect} \rightarrow \text{Verify} \rightarrow \text{Respond}

Not silent agreement. Not submission. The listener accurately carries the other person’s meaning before adding their own.

Someone has to break the symmetry first: I will hold your signal without demanding that mine be carried simultaneously. When that act is reciprocated, talking at becomes thinking with.